body:
`Directory::remove` documents the same relative-path restriction as `Directory::open`: the path must be relative and must not contain `..`. However, [`Directory::open_aux`](https://github.com/moonbitlang/async/blob/441364394f310ad002f42f589b0aca3f5def5835/src/fs/dir.mbt#L310-L343) validates and normalizes the path, while [`Directory::remove_aux`](https://github.com/moonbitlang/async/blob/441364394f310ad002f42f589b0aca3f5def5835/src/fs/dir.mbt#L368-L378) forwards it directly to `@event_loop.remove`.

On Unix native, both `dir.remove("../victim")` and `dir.remove(absolute_path)` can delete a file outside the directory. This is a wrapper-contract inconsistency, not a defect in `unlinkat`: the OS primitive intentionally supports these paths.

A regression test in `src/fs/relative_open_test.mbt`:

```moonbit
#cfg(target="native")
async test "relative remove rejects parent traversal and absolute paths" {
  let root = "_build/relative_remove_validation_test"
  @fs.mkdir("\{root}/base/nested", recursive=true)
  defer @fs.rmdir(root, recursive=true)
  @fs.write_file("\{root}/victim", "keep")
  let absolute = @fs.realpath("\{root}/victim")
  let dir = @fs.opendir("\{root}/base")
  defer dir.close()
  for path in ["../victim", "nested/../../victim", absolute] {
    match @test_util.expect_error_async(() => dir.remove(path)) {
      Failure(_) => ()
      err => raise Failure("expected filename validation failure, got \{err}")
    }
    inspect(@fs.read_file("\{root}/victim").text(), content="keep")
  }
}
```

Run `moon test src/fs --target native --filter 'relative remove rejects*'`. Native execution currently fails with `expected error, but no error is raised`; separate parent-traversal and absolute-path cases both delete the victim.

Please reuse the existing relative filename validator before constructing the remove Job. This also gives removal the same separator and dot-component normalization as relative opening, which is significant for the Windows NT path. Cover a valid path such as `./nested//./file` as well.

This change should remain in the high-level Directory wrapper. It does not turn a directory handle into a sandbox: symlink traversal, concurrent replacement, and authorization at the Wasm host boundary remain separate concerns.

