Opening a file with `mode=ReadOnly, append=true` can request write access on Windows, contrary to the documented meaning of `Mode`.

The Windows branch of [`moonbitlang_async_open_sync`](https://github.com/moonbitlang/async/blob/441364394f310ad002f42f589b0aca3f5def5835/src/internal/event_loop/fs.c#L199-L231) currently does:

```c
if (options->append)
  access_flag = (access_flag ^ GENERIC_WRITE) | FILE_APPEND_DATA;
```

For ReadOnly, the initial mask is `GENERIC_READ`. XOR adds `GENERIC_WRITE` instead of removing it. The resulting mask is `GENERIC_READ | GENERIC_WRITE | FILE_APPEND_DATA`. The public `@fs.open` wrapper accepts this combination and forwards it unchanged.

This can make a read-only open fail when write permission is unavailable, or return a handle that permits writes when they are available. Merely changing XOR to AND-NOT is insufficient: adding `FILE_APPEND_DATA` unconditionally would still grant write access.

Suggested regression test in `src/fs/create_test.mbt`:

```moonbit
async test "append does not grant write access to a read-only file" {
  let path = "_build/append_read_only_test"
  @fs.write_file(path, "keep")
  defer @fs.remove(path)
  let file = @fs.open(path, mode=ReadOnly, append=true)
  defer file.close()
  inspect(file.read_all().text(), content="keep")
  @test_util.assert_raise_async(() => file.write("changed"))
  inspect(@fs.read_file(path).text(), content="keep")
}
```

Run with `moon test src/fs/create_test.mbt --target native` on Windows. Expected: opening and reading succeed, writing fails, and the contents remain unchanged. Windows execution of this reproduction has not been verified; the report is based on the access-mask computation and public call path.

The proposed fix is to apply the append-access transformation only to WriteOnly and ReadWrite modes, using `(access_flag & ~GENERIC_WRITE) | FILE_APPEND_DATA`. ReadOnly keeps its original access. Existing append writes from writable handles must continue to work.

