Observed: test executable exits with signal 11 (SIGSEGV). The OS report places the fault in the main-thread stack guard. The 511 captured frames include the repeating sequence below, matching the original application crash:

```text
Reader::decode_chunk
impl @io.Reader for @http.Reader with _direct_read +564
impl @io.Reader for @http.Reader with _direct_read.*async_driver +5976
impl @io.Reader for @http.Reader with _direct_read +976
impl @io.Reader for @http.Reader with _direct_read.*async_driver +5976
impl @io.Reader for @http.Reader with _direct_read +976
...
```

## Cause and expected behavior

Without gzip, `decode_chunk` returns `(n, n)` from the transport read. EOF therefore yields `(0, 0)`. Both chunked branches leave the remaining length unchanged and recursively call `self._direct_read(...)` when `produced == 0`.

The fixed-length branch already rejects `consumed == 0 && produced == 0` with `@io.ReaderClosed`. The chunked branches should likewise reject premature EOF.

Adding that same guard immediately after `decode_chunk` in both chunked branches, in an isolated copy, makes both bounded tests pass. This must distinguish EOF from valid gzip progress: `consumed > 0, produced == 0` and `consumed == 0, produced > 0` should not be treated as EOF. Replacing recursion with a loop alone would still retry forever on `(0, 0)`.

Related: #427 added premature-EOF validation for fixed-length bodies; #567 / #574 corrected fixed-length gzip EOF detection. The chunked-body no-progress case remains separate.

`mkdir(recursive=true)` is the API for `mkdir -p`, but the already-exists case still raises on every version we tested. Mainstream recursive-mkdir implementations treat an existing directory as success: Rust `create_dir_all`, Go `os.MkdirAll`, Node `mkdirSync { recursive: true }`. The `allow_exist` parameter (new in the 0.22 line) covers it, but it defaults to `false` even when `recursive=true` is set, so the default path of recursive mode still fails on reruns.

## Actual behavior

Linux x86-64, moon 0.1.20260920 (moonc v0.10.14), observed with a standalone package (source below):

| call | 0.21.2 | 0.22.4 |
|---|---|---|
| `mkdir(path, recursive=true)`, leaf does not exist | ok | ok |
| `mkdir(path, recursive=true)`, leaf already exists | raises `OSError(17)` (EEXIST) | raises `OSError(17)` |
| `mkdir(path, recursive=true, allow_exist=true)`, leaf already exists | n/a (no such parameter) | ok |
| recursive walk hitting an already-existing ancestor | raises `OSError(17)` | ok (parents are created with `allow_exist=true` internally) |

Windows surfaces Win32 codes for the same call: in a CI probe (windows-latest, 0.21.2) it fails with `OSError("@fs.mkdir(): \"/tmp\": Cannot create a file when that file already exists.")`, i.e. `ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS` (183). The `is_EEXIST` predicate already maps 80/183 correctly, so on Windows too the raise is the only thing standing between `recursive=true` and `mkdir -p` semantics.

Source-wise: in 0.21.2 `src/fs/dir.mbt` the recursive catch guard is `recursive && err.is_ENOENT()` only, and `moonbitlang_async_mkdir_sync` does not swallow EEXIST on either platform. In 0.22.4 the new `allow_exist` arm checks `is_EEXIST` plus a `kind(path) is Directory` guard, and the parent walk passes `allow_exist=true` internally; the final leaf attempt still forwards the caller's default `allow_exist=false`.

## Expected behavior

`recursive=true` implying leaf-level `allow_exist=true`: an existing directory at the target is success, an existing non-directory is still an error (same as today, and same as `create_dir_all`). If changing the default is a compat concern, documenting the sharp edge prominently would already help; `recursive=true` without `allow_exist` is exactly the combination whose purpose is `mkdir -p`, and it is the one that fails on already-exists.

## Context

We hit this porting zoxide's data-dir initialization to MoonBit (jumbit). On 0.21.2 the only safe pattern is `exists()`-then-`mkdir`, which is a check-then-act race; `allow_exist` is the race-free shape, but requiring it at every call site means every consumer rediscovers the footgun. A Windows CI probe turned one `mkdir` on an existing directory into ~10 cascaded test failures.

