## Summary

Inside an `async fn`, a loop whose body contains an await point is lowered into the
async state machine as a whole. When the await point sits behind a short-circuited
condition that is never true at runtime, the loop still pays the full per-iteration
state-machine cost: ~20 µs per iteration on this machine (267,000 iterations:
0.36 s for the same loop without an await point vs ~5.8 s with one). The only
difference between the two loops is the syntactic presence of the await.

This makes "conditionally await" patterns a hidden 16× cliff: recovering the fast
path currently requires manually splitting the loop into an async and a sync variant.

## Actual behavior

Native target, Linux x86-64, moon 0.1.20260920 (moonc v0.10.14). Reproduced on
both `moonbitlang/async@0.21.2` and `0.22.4`; numbers are consistent across the
two versions (async-lowered loop 5.7–7.3 s across runs). Best of 3 runs after one
warm-up round on 0.22.4:

| loop body | time |
| --- | --- |
| `if i % 3 == 0 { ... }` (no await point) | 0.36 s |
| `if i % 3 == 0 && !exists_like(i) { ... }` (await point, runtime-short-circuited) | 5.69 s |

The await-carrying call is never executed: `exists_mode` is a field of a mutable
global struct set to `false` before the loop, so the short-circuit holds at runtime
while being unprovable at compile time. Per-iteration delta ≈ 20 µs.

## Expected behavior

Ideally, iterations that cannot suspend would not pay state-machine bookkeeping
(or the per-iteration cost would be low enough that a single unreachable await in
a hot loop is not a 16× cliff). If the cost is inherent to the current lowering
design, documenting it prominently would already help — we only found it by
differential profiling: ~2 s of a 2.5 s query path was this tax, hidden behind a
short-circuited boolean.

## Context

We hit this in jumbit (a MoonBit rewrite of zoxide): the query-all hot loop
contained `opts.exists && !exists(dir.path)`. With `--all`, the left operand is
always false at runtime, so the async `exists` call never executes — but the loop
was still lowered, costing ~2 s over 267k entries. Splitting it into two loop
variants (async / pure sync) by the flag removed 2 s of the 2.53 s total.

## Steps to reproduce

